cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "UNITARES: Information-Theoretic Governance of Heterogeneous Agent Fleets"
message: "Prepared v6.9.4 release revision. See README for archive publication status and citation."
type: article
authors:
  - family-names: Wang
    given-names: Kenny
    affiliation: "CIRWEL Systems"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-7544-2374"
version: "v6.9.4"
url: "https://github.com/CIRWEL/unitares-paper-v6"
license: CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >
  UNITARES is a runtime governance layer for heterogeneous AI-agent fleets.
  It updates a four-dimensional state estimate x = (E, I, S, V)^T at each
  check-in and records governance interventions. We describe its
  February–April 2026 deployment, a class-conditional calibration protocol,
  and a migration method for changing reported coordinate semantics while
  preserving the existing decision path.

  The proposed information-theoretic semantics are response-distribution
  entropy (S), context-response mutual information (I), negative
  variational free energy (E), and an accumulated free-energy residual (V).
  These are research targets: the reported deployment uses tier-3 heuristic
  estimators, and the specified logprob and multi-sample estimators remain
  unimplemented. Class-specific healthy operating points and manifold radii
  are measured for five agent classes; the remaining higher-tier scale
  constants are placeholders.

  The empirical record includes a February 20 snapshot of 903 registered
  agents, 75 of them active, and a separate April 18 basin-label
  counterfactual on 13,310 state observations. Replacing the legacy tanh
  coherence with a class-conditional distance score changes 28.9% of basin
  assignments, predominantly into the low basin. The legacy score is nearly
  constant (about 0.49), and labels change where the replacement crosses
  classifier thresholds. This comparison changes both the formula and
  calibration; it does not isolate the value of class conditioning, replay
  the intervention pipeline, or establish which labels are correct. The
  surviving public export is a nearby pull (N = 13,292; 28.8% stored-label
  flips), not an exact archive of that table.

  The migration mechanism orders decision-producing stages before
  presentation-producing enrichment. A corrected contraction certificate
  covers the printed fixed-parameter continuous vector field while
  trajectories remain in its stated domain and share exogenous inputs. It
  does not certify the implemented finite-step update or adaptive governor;
  an offline implementation counterexample illustrates this boundary. The
  paper establishes a framework and bounded descriptive results, not a
  validation of intervention quality or the proposed information-theoretic
  estimators.
keywords:
  - autonomous governance
  - information theory
  - variational free energy
  - heterogeneous multi-agent systems
  - class-conditional calibration
  - AI safety
